# packages/backend — these variables live on the CONVEX DEPLOYMENT, not in a
# file. This one only documents them. Set each with:
#
#   pnpm --filter @astrotan/backend exec convex env set <NAME> <value>
#
# and list what is set with `convex env list`. Never commit real values.

# What: signs Better Auth cookies and derives verification/state tokens.
# Where: generate one with `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# Secret: YES — full session-forgery power.
# If wrong: `createAuth` refuses to start. Its guard rejects a missing value,
#   a value under 32 characters, and Better Auth's publicly-known default —
#   so the placeholder below fails loudly if pasted verbatim.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=CHANGE-ME-not-a-real-secret

# What: public origin of the site that owns the Better Auth session — the
#   DASHBOARD (`apps/admin`), never the public site. Also the base
#   `invitations.ts` builds its `/accept-invite?token=…` links from.
# Where: your dashboard domain (e.g. `https://admin.example.com`).
# Secret: no.
# If wrong: invitation links point at the wrong host. Unset, the scheduled
#   send throws and shows as a failed job in the Convex dashboard.
SITE_URL=http://localhost:3001

# `BETTER_AUTH_SECRET` and `SITE_URL` are only required for auth instances
# that serve HTTP requests (`convex/http.ts`). Regenerating the Better Auth
# schema needs neither: `convex/betterAuth/auth.ts` is an introspection-only
# shim that opts out with `createAuth({} as any, { requireSecret: false })`.
# That is the ONLY place allowed to do so — `grep -rn "requireSecret: false"
# packages/backend` must return exactly that one line.

# What: HMAC key for preview tokens (`convex/lib/previewToken.ts`, spec §6.3).
#   Signs and re-verifies `{type}:{id}:{expiresAt}` for `previewPage`. It is
#   the only thing between a draft page and anyone who guesses a preview URL,
#   since `apps/web` carries no session at all.
# Where: generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32`. Set the SAME value on the
#   `web` side (apps/web/.env.example locally, the VPS `.env` in production).
# Secret: YES.
# If wrong: every preview token is rejected. Unset or under 32 characters,
#   `signPreviewToken`/`verifyPreviewToken` throw instead of degrading.
PREVIEW_SECRET=CHANGE-ME-not-a-real-secret

# What: HMAC key for visitor chat session tokens (lib/chatSessionToken.ts).
# Where: generate with openssl rand -hex 32. SAME value on the web container.
# Secret: YES.
CHAT_SESSION_SECRET=CHANGE-ME-not-a-real-secret

# What: shared secret `convex/revalidate.ts`'s `drain` action sends as the
#   `x-revalidate-secret` header on every POST to
#   `${WEB_SITE_URL}/api/revalidate` (spec §6.2).
# Where: generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32`. Set the SAME value on the
#   `web` side.
# Secret: YES. Never sent to a browser.
# If wrong: `apps/web` refuses every invalidation; `drain` retries with
#   backoff and marks the row `failed` after 6 attempts. Published pages stay
#   stale until the cache expires, with nothing else visibly broken.
REVALIDATE_SECRET=CHANGE-ME-not-a-real-secret

# What: public origin of `apps/web` — where `drain` POSTs its
#   `/api/revalidate` requests. Deliberately NOT the same variable as
#   `SITE_URL` above: that one is Better Auth's `baseURL` and belongs to the
#   dashboard, a different origin entirely.
# Where: your public site domain (e.g. `https://example.com`). Same value as
#   `apps/admin`'s `VITE_WEB_SITE_URL`.
# Secret: no.
# If wrong: invalidations POST to the wrong host and never reach a live page.
#   Unset, `drain` throws rather than silently no-op'ing.
WEB_SITE_URL=http://localhost:4321

# What: Resend API key used by `invitations.ts` to send invitation emails.
# Where: https://resend.com, then `convex env set`.
# Secret: YES.
# If wrong: `resend.sendEmail` throws "API key is not set" from inside the
#   SCHEDULED send, never from `invitations.create` — so a missing key costs
#   you the email, never the invitation itself.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# What: safety rail. The Resend component defaults to `testMode: true`, which
#   only delivers to Resend's test addresses (e.g. delivered@resend.dev), so a
#   misconfigured dev deployment cannot mail real people.
# Where: set to the literal string `false` — and only after verifying a
#   sending domain in the Resend dashboard — to deliver for real.
# Secret: no.
# If wrong: `invitations.ts` reads it as `!== "false"`, so any other value
#   (including unset) keeps test mode and real invitations are never
#   delivered.
RESEND_TEST_MODE=true

# What: shared secret `/api/contact` presents to `leads.submit`. The public
#   site's write door — the ONLY path by which `apps/web` writes to Convex.
# Where: identical here and in the `web` container's environment. Generate
#   with `openssl rand -hex 32`. `pnpm bootstrap` sets both sides.
# Secret: YES. It never reaches the browser; a `PUBLIC_` prefix would amount
#   to not having one.
# If wrong: `leads.submit` refuses every submission, and `/api/contact`
#   redirects to `?erreur=indisponible`. The visitor sees "momentarily
#   unavailable" and nothing explains why — the form is simply dead.
LEAD_SUBMIT_SECRET=

# What: shared secret `/api/consent` presents to `consent.record` — the
#   proof-of-consent log (GDPR art. 7-1).
# Where: identical here and in the `web` container. `pnpm bootstrap` sets
#   both sides.
# Secret: YES.
# If wrong: unset, `/api/consent` answers 204 and writes nothing — the log is
#   silent, not broken, which is the harder failure to notice. Mismatched,
#   `consent.record` refuses and the route swallows it: a person's choice is
#   still applied and stored in their browser, only the proof is lost.
CONSENT_LOG_SECRET=

# What: shared secret the `routeur` service presents to `routing.hotes` —
#   the query that tells it which hosts Traefik must route. That service
#   runs in a container with no account and no Better Auth session, so a
#   shared secret is the only door available; and `settings.get` is public,
#   so `declaredDomain` cannot travel that way.
# Where: identical here and in the `routeur` container's environment
#   (`docker/.env`). Generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`. `pnpm bootstrap`
#   sets both sides.
# Secret: YES.
# If wrong: `routing.hotes` refuses every call and routing stays FROZEN on
#   whatever the file already holds. The site keeps serving — that is the
#   right way round — but changing the domain from the dashboard stops
#   having any effect, and nothing says so outside
#   `docker compose logs routeur`.
ROUTING_SECRET=

# What: the fallback hosts `routing.hotes` returns until someone declares a
#   domain from `/settings/domaine`. That fallback is the NORMAL state of a
#   fresh deployment, not an error.
# Where: your three domains, as bare hosts (`example.com`, no scheme, no
#   port). They live HERE, on the Convex deployment: since Traefik reads a
#   file instead of Docker labels, no container interpolates them any more.
#   `pnpm bootstrap` sets them from the same answers as the VPS `.env`.
# Secret: no.
# If wrong: `WEB_DOMAIN` missing makes the query throw `NOT_CONFIGURED`, so
#   the router writes no routing at all — a site that is up and unreachable,
#   on a deployment whose pipeline is green. `ADMIN_DOMAIN` unset falls back
#   to `admin.<WEB_DOMAIN>`.
#   `UMAMI_DOMAIN` is the odd one: its PRESENCE is what says an Umami
#   dashboard is deployed. Unset, the query returns `umami: null` and no
#   router is written for it. Set for an Umami that does not exist, Traefik
#   asks for a certificate for a name with no DNS record — and every failure
#   counts against the Let's Encrypt quota (five per domain per week).
WEB_DOMAIN=
ADMIN_DOMAIN=
UMAMI_DOMAIN=

# What: IPv4 publique du VPS, celle que les enregistrements A doivent viser.
#   `/settings/domaine` compare le DNS public (Cloudflare DoH) à CETTE
#   valeur — jamais au lookup du domaine déclaré, qui se comparerait à
#   lui-même. Absente : l'écran dit Non connecté (ou Local en dev), il
#   n'invente pas une IP.
# Where: l'onglet IP du panel VPS. `pnpm bootstrap` la pose si `VPS_IP4`
#   est dans `.env.deploy`. Sinon : `convex env set VPS_IP4 <ipv4>`.
# Secret: no.
# If wrong: une IPv4 privée ou malformée est ignorée. Un chiffre faux
#   laisse « Non connecté » tant que le DNS public ne matche pas.
VPS_IP4=

# What: master key for the envelope encryption of tokens typed into the
#   dashboard (`convex/lib/secretsCrypto.ts`). AES-256-GCM, so it must
#   decode to exactly 32 bytes — `openssl rand -base64 32` gives that, and
#   nothing shorter is accepted.
# Where: generate one with the exact command the refusal message and the
#   settings screen both print:
#     cd packages/backend && npx convex env set SECRETS_KEY "$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
#   `pnpm bootstrap` generates it and posts it for you.
# Secret: YES — it decrypts every token below that was typed rather than
#   set here. It must NEVER be settable from the dashboard: it would then
#   be stored in the same place as what it protects.
# If wrong: absent, `secrets.set` throws SECRETS_KEY_MISSING, the settings
#   screen disables its button and shows the command — a clean refusal, but
#   the WHOLE `secrets` family is then inert: the nine tokens below can
#   only be set by `convex env set`, so `/settings/mesure` and
#   `/settings/ia` are decorative on a fresh deployment. Present but
#   malformed (not 32 bytes once decoded) is reported separately, because
#   the remedy is not the same as for absent.
SECRETS_KEY=

# `convex/secrets.ts` closes a list of eleven tokens that may be typed from
# the dashboard. `RESEND_API_KEY` above is one of them; the other ten
# follow. Each can live in EITHER place, and the precedence is decided
# once, in `secrets.ts` and nowhere else: **the environment wins**. A value
# set below is used and the encrypted row in the database is ignored —
# deliberately, so that a token posted by an operator through the CLI is
# not silently overwritten by a form in someone else's session. The
# `secrets.status` query returns `source` for exactly that reason, so a
# screen can say which one is serving rather than a bare "configured".
#
# Leaving them empty here is the normal choice: they are then typed from
# `/settings/mesure` and `/settings/ia`, encrypted under `SECRETS_KEY`
# above. They are listed in this file because a variable the code reads and
# no file documents is a variable nobody sets — `scripts/check-env-wiring.mjs`
# fails on that now.

# What: OpenRouter key, for the dashboard's AI-assisted fields and the
# visitor agent's embeddings (`openai/text-embedding-3-small`, 1536).
# Secret: YES. If absent: the AI screen says so and refuses; RAG reindex
# fails cleanly with OPENROUTER_NOT_CONFIGURED; the chat bubble stays
# « indisponible ». Same key, no second vendor.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# The five halves of the Umami read path. Self-hosted Umami has no API key
# (that is a Cloud-only mechanism): `convex/lib/umamiToken.ts` authenticates
# with `POST /api/auth/login` and a username/password, which returns a JWT.
# So these are the credentials of a UMAMI ACCOUNT, created in Umami's own
# interface — never a variable the compose consumes, and never in the VPS
# `.env` (docker/README.md §13).
#
# What: `UMAMI_API_URL` the dashboard's origin (e.g. `https://stats.example.com`),
#   `UMAMI_API_WEBSITE_ID` the site's uuid as Umami shows it,
#   `UMAMI_API_USERNAME`/`UMAMI_API_PASSWORD` the account,
#   `UMAMI_API_SHARE_ID` the read-only share id, for the "open Umami" link.
# Secret: the password YES; the other four are low-sensitivity but sit in
#   the same table and are treated the same way.
# If wrong or absent: the dashboard's audience panel says the measurement
#   is not configured and shows nothing. The public site is unaffected —
#   its own `PUBLIC_UMAMI_*` are build-time variables of the `web` image,
#   a different pair of halves entirely.
UMAMI_API_URL=
UMAMI_API_WEBSITE_ID=
UMAMI_API_USERNAME=
UMAMI_API_PASSWORD=
UMAMI_API_SHARE_ID=

# What: DataForSEO API login (the account email), for dashboard SEO lookups.
# Secret: YES. If absent: the SEO screen says so and refuses; nothing else.
DATAFORSEO_LOGIN=

# What: DataForSEO API password, paired with DATAFORSEO_LOGIN above.
# Secret: YES. If absent: the SEO screen says so and refuses; nothing else.
DATAFORSEO_PASSWORD=

# What: Google Calendar OAuth client secret, paired with the client id typed
#   on `/settings/agent` (that id is not a secret — it lives in settings
#   getPrivate, never in settings.get). Normal path: paste the secret from
#   the dashboard; it is encrypted under SECRETS_KEY. Env override is the
#   operator escape hatch, same precedence as the other tokens.
# Secret: YES. If absent: Calendar tools stay inert; the agent still answers.
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_CLIENT_SECRET=

# What: Google Calendar OAuth refresh token, written by the
#   `/api/connectors/google/callback` exchange after "Connecter". Do not
#   invent one. Leaving this empty is the normal choice.
# Secret: YES. If absent: free/busy and event creation refuse cleanly.
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# What: test-only stub. When set to the literal `1`, `seoAnalyze.analyze`
#   skips yoastseo (the engine does not load in the edge-runtime used by
#   the permission matrix). The test suite sets it; production must not.
# Secret: no. If set in production: every analysis returns empty findings.
SEO_ANALYZE_STUB=
